    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "would have This is a third conditional sentence , which describes a hypothetical past situation and its result. The structure is: <blockquote> If + past perfect (had + past participle), ... would have + past participle.</blockquote><ul><li> The condition is: \"If she had prepared well\" (past perfect). </li><li> The result clause requires \"would have\" to indicate a certain outcome in the past: \"she would have certainly won the scholarship.\" </li></ul> Why Other Options Are Incorrect: <ul><li> might have → Implies possibility (e.g., \"might have won\" ), but clashes with \"certainly,\" which demands certainty. </li><li> could have → Suggests ability or possibility (e.g., \"could have won if able\" ), but \"certainly\" rules out uncertainty. </li><li> may have → Indicates uncertainty (used for speculation, e.g., \"may have won\" ). It is grammatically weak in third conditionals and conflicts with \"certainly.\" </li></ul>",
