    "id": 43,
    "questionNumber": 43,
    "subject": "Use of English",
    "topic": "English Grammar",
    "subtopic": "Parts of Speech",
    "year": 2025,
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "text": "Choose the word or group of words that best completes each of the following sentences. Passengers are not allowed to carry ...... on this flight.",
    "options": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "a luggage"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "much luggage"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "few luggages"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "many luggage"
      }
    ],
    "optionsMap": {
      "A": "a luggage",
      "B": "much luggage",
      "C": "few luggages",
      "D": "many luggage"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "much luggage \"Luggage\" is an uncountable noun (like furniture or information ). It cannot be pluralized or used with numbers. The correct quantifier for uncountable nouns is \"much\" . Correct Sentence:<blockquote> \"Passengers are not allowed to carry much luggage on this flight.\" </blockquote> Why Other Options Are Incorrect: <ul><li> a luggage → Incorrect. \"Luggage\" cannot take \"a\" (indefinite article) because it is uncountable. </li><li> few luggages → Incorrect. \"Few\" is for countable plural nouns , and \"luggage\" cannot be pluralized. </li><li> many luggage → Incorrect. \"Many\" is used with countable plural nouns (e.g., bags ), not uncountable nouns. </li></ul>",
